# The CLI's second stream (CLAUDE.md §10: the artifact on stdout,
# diagnostics on stderr). New in the port — the bootstrap gets eprintln
# from Rust; the selfhost compiler gets stderr the way any Heroes
# program gets anything: from C (§1.11).
#
# THE SHAPE IS CHOSEN BY THE LANGUAGE'S OWN REFUSALS. stderr the
# OBJECT is exactly what ffi_not_constant exists to refuse (§4.2:
# reading it twice could give two answers), and that diagnostic's note
# names the route: reach C's objects through functions. So the stream
# arrives as a function call whatever is on the other side.
#
# WHICH FUNCTION CHANGED, AND WHY IT IS THE RUNTIME'S (author decision
# 2026-08-24). This module used to bind POSIX's write(2) directly —
# file descriptor 2 IS the error stream, as a number, on every machine
# the fixpoint ran on. The trouble is which machines those were: the
# binding put `#include <unistd.h>` into `seed/heroes.c`, and neither
# MSVC nor clang-cl ships that header, so the self-hosted compiler
# could not be built on Windows at all. Nothing caught it because the
# Rust bootstrap had no such dependency, so the third CI leg was
# testing a compiler that did not have the problem.
#
# The route out is not a platform arm — this language has no `#if`, by
# design — but `hero_write_err` in `runtime/parts/os.c`, over
# `fwrite(…, stderr)`, which is C89 and needs no POSIX. The platform
# question lives in the runtime's C, the one file in this project that
# is allowed to know what machine it is on, and `hero_os.h` is a
# header this tree already binds everywhere else. It also closes a
# §1.12 hole rather than only moving an include: write(2) may write
# FEWER bytes than it was given, and both shipping call sites below
# threw the count away, so a truncated diagnostic was indistinguishable
# from a whole one. `hero_write_err` writes all of it or the process
# has no error channel left, so there is no count left to discard.

extern "hero_os.h"
    function hero_write_err(text: str)
    function hero_word_bits() -> i64

# One line to stderr, newline included — the eprintln the commands
# share.
function eprint(text: str)
    hero_write_err(text: text + "\n")

# How wide C's `long` is on this target, in bits — asked of C rather
# than tabled (author decision 2026-08-26, `/decide` answer `4a`).
#
# **The wrapper is not ceremony**: acceptance row 1 says an `extern` is
# never callable across a module boundary, so the route out of this
# module is a Heroes function beside the declaration. It lives here
# because this is the module that already carries the `hero_os.h`
# group, and a second group in a second module would put the same
# header in two translation units.
#
# What reads it is `emit_c_spellings.spelling`, which tells an author
# which Heroes type a C `long` is. That file's own comment has said
# since the port that *"a width is not a constant to be tabled; it is a
# question about the target"* — and every live call site then passed
# the literal 64, which is the one option the same comment calls
# forbidden. 64 is right on Darwin and Linux and wrong on Windows,
# where `long` is 32 bits.
function word_bits() -> i64
    return hero_word_bits()

# The same channel, no newline added — for a blob that already ends
# with one (the rich renderer's, the JSON's).
function eprint_raw(text: str)
    hero_write_err(text: text)

# An ARTIFACT printed exactly as it is. `print` adds exactly one
# trailing newline (spec § Built-ins), and every blob this compiler
# hands to stdout — a dump, the formatted file, the generated C —
# already ends with one, so printing it plainly grows a blank line at
# the end. The bootstrap's `print!` adds nothing, so the two disagreed
# on one byte per invocation: measured 2026-08-17 against the corpus,
# and on `--emit-c` that one byte is the fixpoint.
function print_artifact(text: str)
    cs = text.chars()

    if cs.len() > 0 && cs[cs.len() - 1] == "\n"
        print(cs.slice(from: 0, to: cs.len() - 1).join(""))
        return
    print(text)

## Tests

test "the error channel takes every shape a diagnostic arrives in"
    # THERE IS NO COUNT TO ASSERT, and that is the repair rather than a
    # gap in this test: `hero_write_err` returns nothing because the
    # count `write(2)` used to return was discarded at two of three
    # call sites, which made a truncated diagnostic look like a whole
    # one. What replaces the assertion is an instrument that already
    # exists and is far larger than this file: every
    # `tests/golden/check/` case compares the diagnostic text that
    # leaves through here BYTE FOR BYTE — 65 `check` plus 76
    # `annotations` checks in the net — so a short write is 141 red
    # checks, not a silent missing tail.
    #
    # What is checked here is the shapes NEXT TO the one that provoked
    # the change (CLAUDE.md §1): the empty blob, which must not reach
    # `fwrite` at all; bytes that are not characters, where a length in
    # the wrong unit would truncate mid-sequence; a blob that already
    # ends in a newline, which is `eprint_raw`'s whole reason; and one
    # past the stdio buffer, where a write is split into more than one
    # call to the platform.
    #
    # A GENUINE partial write cannot be forced from inside this
    # language — it needs a full pipe or a closing reader — so the long
    # case here crosses the buffer boundary and no more. That is why
    # the 141 byte-comparing checks above are named as the instrument
    # and this test is not: a test that cannot fail is not evidence.
    eprint_raw(text: "")
    eprint(text: "eprint says hello during the suite — this line is the test")
    eprint(text: "multi-byte: ziggy ★ stardust — 3-byte and 4-byte: 𝅘𝅥𝅮")
    eprint_raw(text: "already ends with its own newline\n")
    long: str @ "buffer-crossing ".repeat(count: 512)
    eprint_raw(text: long + "\n")

    # The bytes are what the channel is measured in, not the characters
    # — the one place a wrong unit would cut a sequence in half.
    assert "ziggy ★".len() == 9
    assert long.len() == 8192
